**Read:** Follow the complete [Design and Manufacturing II — MIT 2.008, 2025](../sources/Design-and-Manufacturing-II-MIT-2025.lingua#r_2FHEWYSEAHBY7X6030TW00F32E) lectures, available homework and examinations as a sustained manufacturing foundation. Use the project as a worked example of iteration between geometry, tools, production and measured parts.

A process chain turns feedstock into a useful part through changes of shape, structure and surface. Choose it with material, size, tolerance, quantity, equipment, skills, inspection and service in view. A near-net blank may reduce cutting while requiring tooling and control of shrinkage or defects.

Write the state of the part before and after each operation. Include setup references, stock allowance, handling, heat treatment, cleaning, inspection and possible rework. A final precision operation cannot recover a hidden crack or an inaccessible feature merely by meeting its outer dimensions.

**Work:** Create two process chains for the same support or shaft: one for a single item and another for a recurring batch. Explain each transformation and the evidence that it achieved the intended state. Identify irreversible operations and inspections that must occur before later work hides the feature.

**Return:** [Mechanical engineering and machine design](../Mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design.lingua#r_0017YZ63HZBZ4P4WD8DECVWE4R).
**Continue:** [Stock, blanks, material condition and recoverable yield](Stock-blanks-material-condition-and-recoverable-yield.lingua#r_10JN4N3M6T40VXEHKARYSF4M3V).

